## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to model task dependencies as a DAG and compute an optimal parallel schedule using topological ordering and dynamic programming.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological sort**: Ordering nodes such that all prerequisites come before dependent tasks
- **DAG DP**: Computing the longest path in a directed acyclic graph
- **Cycle detection**: Using indegree counts to identify cycles (if not all nodes are processed)
- **Time complexity**: O(n + m) where n is nodes and m is edges

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinish(n, edges, duration) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const finish = duration.slice();
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  }

  let processed = 0;

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + duration[v]);
      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }

  if (processed !== n) return -1;
  return Math.max(...finish);
}
```

## Key Takeaways

- Unlimited workers means tasks run in parallel when dependencies allow
- The finish time of a task is the maximum of (prerequisite finish + its own duration)
- The pipeline completes when the slowest path (critical path) finishes

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in per-prefix top-K maintenance using a Trie with bounded heaps and dynamic frequency updates, revealing trade-offs between speed and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node top-K storage using a min-heap with bounded size K
- Min-heap with tie-breaking by word for stable ordering
- Dynamic frequency updates with addWord(word, delta) supporting negative deltas
- Automatic deletion when frequency reaches zero

## Time and Memory Trade-offs
- **Time Complexity**: addWord runs in O(L × log K) where L is word length, as we traverse the path and perform heap operations. getTopK runs in O(K × log K) for heap extraction plus O(K log K) for sorting.
- **Space Complexity**: O(N × L + N × K) where N is the total number of unique words—the Trie itself costs O(N × L), and each node stores up to K heap entries.
- **Trade-off**: Increasing K improves result quality but increases memory usage and slows updates. A smaller K is faster but returns fewer suggestions.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.topK = new MinHeap((a, b) => {
      if (a.freq !== b.freq) return a.freq - b.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });
  }
}

class AutocompleteEngine {
  constructor(K = 3) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.freqMap = new Map();
    this.K = K;
  }

  addWord(word, delta) {
    const newFreq = (this.freqMap.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    if (newFreq <= 0) {
      this.freqMap.delete(word);
    } else {
      this.freqMap.set(word, newFreq);
    }

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.topK.upsert({ word, freq: newFreq }, this.K);
    }
  }

  getTopK(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTop(K);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a stable multi-key sort and apply bit-operations in a practical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Two-key comparator
- Hamming weight (popcount)
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortByWeightAndValue(arr) {
  const popcount = (n) => {
    n = n >>> 0;
    n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
    n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
    n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
    return (n * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
  };
  const items = arr.map(v => ({ val: v, w: popcount(v) }));
  const mergeSort = (a) => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a.slice();
    const m = Math.floor(a.length / 2);
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i].w !== R[j].w) res.push(L[i].w < R[j].w ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
      else res.push(L[i].val <= R[j].val ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
    }
    while (i < L.length) res.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) res.push(R[j++]);
    return res;
  };
  const sorted = mergeSort(items);
  return sorted.map(o => o.val);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question adds counting of optimal partitions to a standard min-max DP, testing both correctness and edge-case handling in a realistic workload-balancing scenario.

## Key Concepts

- DP with a value-cost pair (minCost, count) and prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation with identical transitions
- Edge-case handling: n<k invalid, zeros, large sums

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostPartitions(nums, k){
  const n = nums.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+nums[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array.from({length:k+1},()=>null));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = {best: pref[i], count:1};
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let j=2;j<=Math.min(i,k);j++){
      let best = Infinity, cnt = 0;
      for(let t=j-1;t<=i-1;t++){
        const last = pref[i]-pref[t];
        const prev = dp[t][j-1];
        if(!prev) continue;
        const cand = Math.max(prev.best, last);
        if(cand < best){ best = cand; cnt = prev.count; }
        else if(cand === best){ cnt += prev.count; }
      }
      dp[i][j] = {best, count: cnt};
    }
  }
  const res = dp[n][k];
  return res ? {cost: res.best, ways: res.count} : null;
}
```

